statement:
You are given an integer `n`, two strings `s1` and `s2` of length `n` with `s1 <= s2` in dictionary order, and a string `evil`. All of them use lowercase English letters only.

Call a string **good** when all of these hold:

- its length is exactly `n`;
- in dictionary order it is not smaller than `s1` and not larger than `s2` (both ends count);
- `evil` does not appear anywhere inside it as a contiguous substring.

Return how many good strings exist. The count can be very large, so return it modulo `10^9 + 7`.

validator:
function validate([n, s1, s2, evil]) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1 || n > 500) return 'n is a whole number from 1 to 500.';
  if (s1.length !== n || s2.length !== n) return 's1 and s2 must both have length n.';
  if (evil.length < 1 || evil.length > 50) return 'evil has 1 to 50 letters.';
  if (![s1, s2, evil].every(s => /^[a-z]+$/.test(s))) return 'Use lowercase English letters only.';
  if (s1 > s2) return 's1 must not come after s2 in dictionary order.';
  return null;
}

